---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const description =
  'Re-runs reap the previous run. Failures always land in the trace. No-op re-runs are green. WAL sidecars stay untracked.';
---
<DocsLayout
  title="Run semantics"
  description={description}
  current="/docs/run-semantics"
  prev={{ href: '/docs/configuration', label: 'Configuration' }}
  next={{ href: '/docs/sandbox', label: 'Sandboxed runs' }}
>
  <p>
    The factory runs the same chains over and over. The semantics below are what make the
    thousandth run look like the first, and they are enforced in code, not promised in prompts.
  </p>

  <h2>Re-runs reap the previous run</h2>
  <p>
    Running an ADW again with the same <code class="hl">--adw-id</code> terminates any processes
    the previous run left in flight (the pid is verified against the recorded command first, so a
    recycled pid is spared) and marks its open phases and session <code class="hl">failed</code>.
    A stalled or killed run never lingers as <code class="hl">running</code> in the trace.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because the session is <em>joined</em> rather than recreated, each agent resumes its existing
    context window through <code class="hl">agent_map.json</code>: the re-run is a continuation,
    not a cold start.
  </p>

  <h2>Failures always land in the trace</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Per-phase exceptions</strong> are recorded by the phase manager: the phase goes
      <code class="hl">failed</code>, the session is marked, and the run exits non-zero.</li>
    <li><strong>Anything else</strong>: an uncaught exception anywhere in the ADW process is
      caught by a failsafe hook that marks the session failed before the process exits.</li>
    <li><strong>A killed run</strong> finalizes its own trace to <code class="hl">fail</code> when
      the process ends.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    There is no silent failure: if it crashed, the trace says so, and the trace is the single
    source of truth the visualizer, the terminal commands, and the healer all read.
  </p>

  <h2>No-op re-runs are green</h2>
  <p>
    If <code class="hl">commit_build</code> finds nothing to commit and the builder reported no
    changes, the work is already implemented and verified: the run succeeds with a note and skips
    the document chain. The factory distinguishes &ldquo;already done&rdquo; from &ldquo;did
    nothing&rdquo;:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Builder reports no changed files + suite green + review approved → success, no-op, no doc update.</li>
    <li>Builder <em>claims</em> changed files that are not in the working tree → hard failure. A
      claim that never landed is not a no-op; it is a lie the diff gate exists to catch.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>WAL sidecars stay untracked</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="cmd">sssf init</span> gitignores <code class="hl">sssf.db-wal</code> /
    <code class="hl">sssf.db-shm</code>. If they ever get committed, agents treat them as clutter
    and may <code class="hl">git checkout</code> them over the live db, which breaks open reader
    connections (<code class="hl">SQLITE_IOERR_VNODE</code>) and makes the visualizer go blank.
    Keep them untracked; the db is rebuilt from the raw files if it is ever lost.
  </p>

  <h2>Success must be earned: the finish contract</h2>
  <p>
    Every phase defaults to <code class="hl">fail</code>; a clean exit flips it, and an agent phase
    also needs its envelope to parse and every gate green. Then
    <code class="hl">run.finish(accepted=...)</code> decides the run-level verdict:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code class="hl">accepted=True</code> → exit 0, session <code class="hl">success</code>, green banner.</li>
    <li><code class="hl">accepted=False</code> → non-zero exit, session <code class="hl">failed</code>, red banner.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Phases passing is not the same as the run being acceptable: a test phase that ran a red suite
    did its job perfectly. The exit code, the session status, and the banner are decided in one
    call so they cannot disagree.
  </p>

  <h2>Run control</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Command</th><th>Semantics</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code class="hl">sssf run stop &lt;adw_id&gt;</code></td><td>Kill a live run: the sandbox is torn down and the run is marked failed.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code class="hl">sssf run restart &lt;adw_id&gt;</code></td><td>Re-run a session on its existing branch with the original request as the prompt: the ADW joins and reaps the old state.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Observing from the terminal</h2>
  <pre><code>sssf sessions        # recent runs: adw_id · name · status · request · tokens · cost
sssf phases &lt;id&gt;     # the phase waterfall of one run
sssf tail &lt;id&gt;       # the last 25 events of one run, newest first
sssf procs &lt;id&gt;      # live processes of one run (adw_id → pid)</code></pre>
  <p>
    All four resolve the project db (or take <code class="hl">--project</code>), open it WAL, and
    render a rich table. Reads never block a running workflow.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
